# XSH fwrite()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.fwrite.001
anchor     functions/fwrite.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fwrite.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       On a "w" stream, fwrite(obj, 4, 2, f) with obj the 8 bytes 0x00
           0xff 0x80 0x0a 0x41 0x00 0x7f 0xfe returns 2, ftell() is then 8,
           and after fclose() the file holds exactly those 8 bytes in that
           order.
note       Issue 6 added the rule that each object's bytes go out in
           order through an unsigned char array overlaying it.
test       t/001-bytes-in-order.c

assertion  XSH.fwrite.002
anchor     functions/fwrite.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       On an "r+" stream on "0123456789", after two fgetc() calls and
           fseek(f, 0, SEEK_CUR), fwrite("AB", 1, 2, f) returns 2 and ftell()
           is 4; after fseek(f, 0, SEEK_CUR) the next fgetc() returns '4',
           and after fclose() the file holds "01AB456789".
note       The bytes go where the file position indicator is (as by
           fputc(), on a stream not opened for append).
test       t/002-at-position.c

assertion  XSH.fwrite.003
anchor     functions/fwrite.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       On a "w" stream on a regular file, fwrite(b, 10, 30000, f) of a
           300000-byte pattern returns 30000, and after fclose() the file
           holds the 300000 bytes.
note       The count may be short only for a write error.
test       t/003-large.c

assertion  XSH.fwrite.004
anchor     functions/fwrite.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       With size 0 or nitems 0, fwrite() returns 0 and the stream is
           unchanged: on a "w" stream after fputs("ab"), both calls leave
           ftell() 2 and ferror() 0, and after fclose() the file holds "ab";
           on a stream with the error indicator set it stays set.
note       The change history does not say which edition added the rule
           (Austin Group defect 1196 reworded it in Issue 8), so "since" is
           left out. The error indicator is set by a flush to a closed
           descriptor, which is then restored with dup2().
test       t/004-zero.c

assertion  XSH.fwrite.005
anchor     functions/fwrite.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fputc.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
says       On a "w" stream whose descriptor has been closed with close(),
           fwrite(b, 1, 1048576, f) returns less than 1048576, sets the error
           indicator, and sets errno to EBADF.
note       The page refers to fputc() for its errors, which apply when the
           buffer needs to be flushed: 1 MiB is written so that it does.
           A stream that takes in the whole MiB without writing (no
           buffer size is set anywhere) makes the test UNRESOLVED; a full
           count with the error indicator set is a FAIL. close() does not
           move the offset, so the descriptor is not a second handle (XSH
           2.5.1). The change history does not say which edition made
           errno required, so "since" is left out.
test       t/005-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.fwrite.006
anchor     functions/fwrite.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/fputc.html#ERRORS/EPIPE
class      required
since      2001
says       On a stream from fdopen() of a pipe's write end whose read end is
           closed, with SIGPIPE blocked, fwrite(b, 1, 1048576, f) returns less
           than 1048576, sets the error indicator and errno to EPIPE, and
           SIGPIPE is then pending.
note       Issue 6 marks EPIPE as a CX error. As for XSH.fwrite.005, 1 MiB
           is written so that the buffer needs to be flushed; a stream that
           takes it all in without writing makes the test UNRESOLVED.
test       t/006-epipe.c

assertion  XSH.fwrite.007
anchor     functions/fwrite.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       On a "w" stream on an existing file, fwrite("abc", 1, 3, f) and
           fflush() (2 s after the stream was opened) leave the file's last
           data modification and status change times later than they were
           after the open.
note       The times are marked between the fwrite() and the completion of
           the next fflush(), and fstat() is an update point (XBD 4.12).
           Issue 7 reworded the rule for fine-grained timestamps; the
           change history does not say when it was added, so "since" is
           left out. The timestamp resolution is implementation-defined;
           the 2 s wait is a practical cap that covers whole-second
           timestamps.
test       t/007-times.c
